Fix the APK build system completely. The current implementation incorrectly depends on manually installing standalone tools such as aapt2, d8, and apksigner through Termux, which causes the build to fail when those tools are unavailable. Replace this fragile approach with a reliable Android project build pipeline similar to the workflow used by mobile web-development apps such as HopWeb: treat the selected project as a web project, automatically package it into a proper Android application project, use a bundled or automatically configured Gradle/Android build environment where possible, automatically detect and configure the required Java JDK and Android SDK components, and then build a real signed installable APK. Do not require the user to manually install individual commands such as aapt2, d8, apksigner, zipalign, or manually locate android.jar before every build.

The app should first detect whether a valid Android build environment is available. If it is available, automatically use it. If required components are missing, provide an automatic setup process from inside the app/Termux integration that installs or configures the complete compatible Android build environment instead of simply displaying a list of missing binaries. Use a consistent Android SDK location, configure ANDROID_HOME/ANDROID_SDK_ROOT correctly, install the required SDK platform and build-tools versions, configure OpenJDK, and verify Gradle before starting the build. The build system must use the Android Gradle Plugin and Gradle wrapper versions that are mutually compatible with the selected JDK and Android SDK. Do not assume that packages named aapt2, d8, or apksigner installed through Termux are the correct Android SDK build tools; obtain these tools from the appropriate Android SDK/Gradle toolchain.

Implement a HopWeb-style “Build APK” workflow for HTML/CSS/JavaScript projects. The user should only need to select the project directory and tap “Build APK.” The system should automatically create a temporary Android wrapper project around the selected web project, copy or reference the web assets correctly, configure the application ID, application name, version information, launcher icon, WebView configuration, required permissions, and Gradle files, then run the Android build process. Keep the generated Android wrapper separate from the user's original source files so the original project is not damaged. After the build succeeds, copy the final APK into the selected project directory, preferably under a clear folder such as build/apk/, refresh the project explorer, and show an “Install APK” button.

The generated APK must be a genuine Android APK rather than a ZIP renamed to .apk or an incomplete artifact. Automatically sign the APK using a development/debug keystore for easy local installation, or use a properly configured release keystore when the user chooses release mode. Automatically perform the required signing and alignment steps through the Android Gradle build system. Validate the final APK before reporting success, including checking that the APK exists, has the correct package structure, is signed, and can be passed to Android's package installer. If the build fails, display the actual Gradle/build error and never create or report a fake APK.

Add a robust Android installation workflow. After a successful build, allow the user to tap “Install APK,” and launch Android's standard package installer using a secure FileProvider URI. Handle Android's “Install unknown apps” permission correctly when required and guide the user through the system permission screen instead of attempting an unsafe silent installation. Also provide “Open APK Location” and “Share APK” actions using Android's native APIs.

Most importantly, redesign the Termux integration so Termux is used as the build environment rather than requiring the web app to directly depend on randomly installed individual binaries. Automatically prepare the required project/build environment, use the Android SDK command-line tools and Gradle wrapper correctly, cache SDK/build dependencies so they do not need to be downloaded for every build, and show setup/build progress inside the app. The user experience should be: select Project Directory → tap “Build APK” → automatic environment check → automatic setup if needed → package the HTML project into an Android project → Gradle build → sign APK → validate APK → save APK inside the selected project directory → show APK → tap “Install APK” → Android installer opens. Make the implementation reliable on Android phones, avoid the current aapt2/d8/apksigner/android.jar missing failure, do not falsely claim a build succeeded, and preserve the existing Codex-style mobile coding interface and project-folder workflow.
